# Source for the Front's heterogeneous coalition: Fakhreddin Azimi,
# Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953, ch. 5, pp. 250–267.
# The recurring fundraising drive and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Fundraising
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: party_affairs
view-if: fundraising_timer <= 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; fundraising_timer = 4

= Fundraising

Printing, travel, meeting rooms, and legal work all draw from the same limited
fund. The coalition can widen its base of contributors, ask a smaller circle
for larger sums, or use its press network to stage a public subscription.

Money does not arrive without a political relationship. Choose which
relationship the secretariat will cultivate.

- @fund_small
- @fund_notables
- @fund_subscription

@fund_small
title: Seek many small organizational contributions
subtitle: Many modest subscriptions keep the campaign independent, though the treasury fills slowly
on-arrival: resources += 2; organizational_reach += 2; independent_nationalists_organization += 2

Local volunteers assemble many small contributions and report where new
supporters can be contacted again. The total is limited, but the collection
itself leaves behind useful lists and habits.

The treasury improves gradually, and independent organizers gain a broader
base that is not tied to one benefactor.

- @post_event: Continue

@fund_notables
title: Approach sympathetic notables
subtitle: A few large gifts fill the treasury quickly and invite questions about whom the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> serves
on-arrival: resources += 4; public_mandate -= 1; iran_party_relation += 2; toilers_dissent += 2

Established intermediaries secure several larger contributions quickly. {!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Iran
Party{!</span>!} contacts appreciate the practical result, but organizers on the social
left ask what access the donors will expect in return.

The treasury is healthier. The coalition's public independence is a little
harder to explain.

- @post_event: Continue

@fund_subscription
title: Run a public subscription through friendly papers
subtitle: The newspapers turn giving into a public act, producing less money but a wider sense of ownership
on-arrival: resources += 3; press_capacity += 2; public_mandate += 1

Friendly papers publish a time-limited appeal and direct readers to local
collectors. The subscription raises less than a concentrated approach to
wealthy supporters, but it turns the request for money into a demonstration of
visible participation.

Editors and collectors learn to coordinate more closely, and the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} can
describe the fund as publicly supported.

- @post_event: Continue
